\title{ACTS-Attack: Demonstrated Capabilities and Boundaries\\in Agent-Executed Cryptographic Attacks}
\author{}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Can language-model agents turn known cryptographic weaknesses into
verified attacks, and what conditions bound that capability?
ACTS-Attack studies code construction, execution and revision against
RC4 keystream reuse, ChaCha20 nonce reuse, an AES-CBC padding oracle
and ECDSA nonce reuse. In a complete twelve-session prospective cohort,
eight candidates are accepted: RC4 0/3, ChaCha20 2/3, CBC 3/3 and ECDSA 3/3.
The transcripts retain 31 submitted programs. Four sessions succeed on
the first submission and four after feedback. ECDSA recovers a reserved
private scalar and signs a challenge in all three first submissions;
CBC recovers plaintext after earlier execution failures, with
20,737--21,508 cumulative oracle queries per session.
Stream-task failures expose an undisclosed generator condition,
preventing interpretation as a ranking of cryptographic difficulty.
A separate deduplicated historical population retains 42 accepted
verdicts among 74 sessions. Fixed operations pass 12/12 controls;
45 local handshakes establish a server-policy enforcement contrast.
Side-channel and escrow cases retain exact and partial recoveries
under their specific leakage and access conditions.
Offline reanalysis checks candidate digests, submitted-code identities
and copied sessions. Distinct populations, missing duration endpoints
and unenforced secret isolation bound latency and autonomy claims.
The results characterize task-conditioned attack execution and revision,
rather than new primitive breaks or unknown-vulnerability discovery.
\end{abstract}
\begin{IEEEkeywords}
AI agents, cryptographic attacks, nonce reuse, padding oracles,
attack construction, capability evaluation
\end{IEEEkeywords}

